item.content:encoded:
When editing code in a modern IDE or editor, developers rely on its refactoring features to automate common transformations. Today, we are excited to introduce in gopls, powered by a new capability we are proposing for the Language Server Protocol (LSP) that brings dynamic, multi-step dialogs directly into your favorite editor. Crucially, we designed this capability (in collaboration with the Dart team) not just for Go, but as an open standard for all programming languages and editor ecosystems. Working support is already available in gopls v0.24.0 and the official Go extension for VS Code v0.56.0.What is the Language Server Protocol (LSP)?The LSP is an open standard that decouples language-specific features from specific text editors. As you navigate and edit code, your editor (such as VS Code, Neovim, or Emacs) and a language server (like gopls) engage in a continuous JSON-RPC exchange to provide features like autocomplete, hover documentation, diagnostics, and code actions.Today, almost every code transformation in the LSP is like a function of no parameters: you trigger the operation, and the server immediately computes the edits. (Renaming a symbol is the sole exception: because it is a function of one parameter, the desired new name, it required its own dedicated RPC in the protocol.)The language server must deduce the user’s intent from context alone, namely the cursor position and the enclosing syntax tree. When that context is not enough, the server is forced into one of two choices: apply a one-size-fits-all default, or refrain from offering the transformation altogether. The limitation lies not in gopls’s ability to compute the necessary edits, but in the LSP’s lack of a mechanism for language servers to ask the user for input.For many transformations, this parameterless model works well. But many others need additional input from the user: which struct tags to add and in what naming convention, which interface to implement, which file to move a declaration to. Without a way to ask, gopls could only guess—“Add struct tags”, for example, always produced tags in —or not offer the refactoring at all. With interactive refactoring, language servers can now easily provide new operations with one or more parameters.While some language extensions have worked around this limitation by embedding custom, language-specific dialog logic directly into individual editors, doing so leaves behind other editors (such as Neovim and Emacs) that stick strictly to the LSP with minimal custom client code. More importantly, editor-specific workarounds go against the fundamental purpose of the LSP: to decouple language-specific features from editors so that each server improvement benefits every client.Interactive refactorings in actionAdding and removing struct tags (gopls v0.23.0)When configuring serialization formats, developers frequently need custom tags and casing conventions. Previously, running “Add struct tags” unconditionally injected tags using formatting.With interactive refactoring:Place your cursor on any struct definition and select the code action.An input dialog appears, pre-filled with , allowing you to customize any combination of tags (such as ).A second dialog lets you select your desired naming convention (such as , , or ).Gopls generates and applies the formatted struct tags across your type definition.Here is the before-and-after transformation:type UserProfile struct { ID int64 FirstName string LastName string EmailAddr string } type UserProfile struct { ID int64 `json:"id" db:"id"` FirstName string `json:"first_name" db:"first_name"` LastName string `json:"last_name" db:"last_name"` EmailAddr string `json:"email_addr" db:"email_addr"` } Similarly, now asks you for the specific tag keys you wish to delete (for example, removing only tags while preserving the tag), rather than wiping out all struct tags indiscriminately.Implementing interfaces with dynamic symbol searchWhen creating a type to satisfy an interface, developers previously had to copy the interface declaration and manually turn it into a set of methods on the receiver type (or rely on external tools like by manually typing the receiver and qualifying the name of the interface).Since gopls v0.23.0, with interactive refactoring:Place your cursor on any concrete type and select the “Add methods to X to implement an interface…” code action.A text entry field appears, inviting you to choose the name of an interface type. It makes dynamic queries to gopls to auto-complete names of types in your workspace and the standard library (for example, ).If the selected interface has type parameters, gopls asks for a concrete type argument for each type parameter (such as ).Gopls generates complete method stubs with appropriate receiver names and instantiated method signatures.type Foo struct{}
type MyHasher struct{} type Foo struct{}
type MyHasher struct{}
// Equal implements [maphash.Hasher]. func (m *MyHasher) Equal(x Foo, y Foo) bool { panic("unimplemented") }
// Hash implements [maphash.Hasher]. func (m *MyHasher) Hash(*maphash.Hash, Foo) { panic("unimplemented") } Moving declarations across files and packages (preview)Moving declarations manually by cutting and pasting can easily break references and requires tedious import cleanup across multiple files. While scheduled for an upcoming gopls release, our working implementation already demonstrates how interactive file selection streamlines cross-file and cross-package moves:Place your cursor on any top-level declaration and select the code action.A file picker dialog lets you choose an existing destination file or specify a new file path.Gopls moves the declaration along with its dependent declarations (such as unexported helpers or methods) while leaving unrelated symbols in place, updates references across your workspace, and cleans up imports in both the source and destination files.In the example below, we move the type from package to package . Its method and the unexported constant , which only uses, move along with it, while the unrelated function stays behind:// from/from.go package from
import ( "fmt" "strings" )
const bar = "bar"
// Doc comment for Foo type Foo struct{}
func (f Foo) Print() { fmt.Println(bar) }
func Baz() string { return strings.ToUpper("baz") } // from/from.go package from
import "strings"
func Baz() string { return strings.ToUpper("baz") }
// to/to.go package to
import "fmt"
const bar = "bar"
// Doc comment for Foo type Foo struct{}
func (f Foo) Print() { fmt.Println(bar) } Slaying the dragon (gopls v0.24.0)As a demonstration of multi-step interactive dialogs and a self-contained test case for client editor implementations, gopls v0.24.0 offers a code action when you place your cursor on a package-level variable named (for example, ). Happy hunting!Interactive refactoring establishes a foundational capability in gopls, opening up several exciting directions on our roadmap:Change signature refactoring (#38028): Adding, removing, or reordering function parameters across a workspace requires input that cannot be deduced from syntax alone, such as the new parameter order or the default argument expression to insert at existing call sites. Interactive forms allow gopls to collect these details before rewriting the declaration and all call sites.Interactive conflict resolution: When a code transformation encounters naming collisions, identifier shadowing, or ambiguous references, gopls can ask the user for guidance, such as asking for permission to proceed or offering to rename conflicting symbols on the fly.Interactive commands beyond code edits: Because interactivity is modeled at the LSP command layer, it naturally generalizes to non-edit server operations that require user configuration, such as configuring test or benchmark runs with profiling flags (CPU, memory, tracing).Getting Started and Sharing FeedbackInteractive refactoring is available in gopls v0.24.0 and v0.56.0 (with proof-of-concept client integrations also explored for Neovim and Emacs).We would love to hear your thoughts as we continue refining interactive code actions in gopls:(Special thanks to Alan Donovan, Alex Putman, Brian Wilkerson, Danny Tuppeny, Hana Kim, Madeline Kalil, Peter Weinberger, and Robert Findley for their insights, discussions, feedback, and collaboration on this work.)

Take a look at the following function:func getStatus(url string) (int, error) {
    resp, err := http.Get(url)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    defer resp.Body.Close()
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}
It sends a  request to  and returns the HTTP status code.Using HTTP/1 (), calling this function 100 times against
index.golang.org takes ~710 milliseconds on Go 1.26
on my machine. On Go 1.27, the same program takes ~150 milliseconds.That is almost 5 times faster! How is such a drastic performance difference
possible?The cost of establishing HTTP connectionsTo understand the difference, consider what happens on the network when we make
an HTTP request.Go’s  package supports HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, and
HTTP/2. All three run over TCP, and usually over TLS when talking to HTTPS
endpoints. Making a request over a new connection looks something like this:: the client sends a  packet, the server
replies with , and the client replies with . Because the
client can send the first TLS message alongside that final ,
establishing the TCP connection takes 1 round-trip time (RTT).: next, a TLS handshake exchanges encryption keys
and verifies the server’s identity; using TLS 1.2, this takes 2 RTT.: with the keys exchanged, HTTP requests can finally be sent
after a total of 3 RTT.These round trips (plus some CPU time spent on TLS handshakes) add up quickly.
In cross-region communications, one-way latencies often reach or exceed the 50
ms (100 ms RTT) shown in the diagram above. Compared to the 300 ms spent just
setting up the connection, the HTTP request itself can be cheap to serve, taking
only a little over 1 RTT when bandwidth is plentiful or the HTTP response is
small.Even in more favorable conditions, opening a new connection can still be
expensive. Our opening benchmark queries
index.golang.org, which is distributed worldwide and
supports TLS 1.3. This means that latency is relatively low, and the TLS
handshake can actually finish in just 1 RTT. Even so, the performance difference
is still very noticeable.To avoid paying this setup cost on every request, HTTP clients and servers try
to reuse existing connections across multiple requests when possible. The
mechanics of this differ based on the HTTP version:: while not part of the official specification, clients and
servers often opt into connection reuse by sending a  header. Reuse continues until either endpoint omits the header
or sends a  header.: connections are persistent by default, and can be reused for
subsequent requests and responses unless either side declares otherwise by
sending a  header.: multiple requests and responses can be multiplexed over a single
TCP connection.When connection reuse failsIn HTTP/1 (both HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1), multiple requests and responses on the
same connection are sent sequentially on the wire. If two  requests are
sent to index.golang.org over the same TCP
connection, the responses arrive as a single stream of bytes:As shown in the diagram,  reads and parses
the status line and headers from the byte stream to populate
, then returns immediately. The
response body remains unread on the connection and is consumed only as the
caller reads from .Because responses are sent back-to-back on the same stream,
 can only find the start of the next response after
the current response body has been read to the very end (as determined by the
 or  header).Now look back at our example program, where we never read :func getStatus(url string) (int, error) {
    resp, err := http.Get(url)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    defer resp.Body.Close()
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}
Through Go 1.26, when  is called, 
sees that there is still an unread response body. Realizing that it cannot reuse
the connection, it abandons the existing connection and creates new connections
for its next requests, causing it to repeatedly incur the cost of establishing
new connections.(HTTP/2 does not have this problem. Because it multiplexes streams of frames
over a single connection rather than sending responses strictly back-to-back, an
unread response body on one stream does not block other requests from sharing
the connection.)Where failed connection reuse hurtsThis subtlety of HTTP/1 connection reuse can cause silent performance
degradation in seemingly innocuous code.As in our opening example, a client might only care about an API response’s
status code. However, the API endpoint it is talking to might send bodies that
provide more detailed but ultimately unused information, such as {"success": true, "message": "update processed"}. This will unfortunately cause connection
reuse to fail.Even programs that do read the response body can run into this issue if they
stop just short of the end of the body. For example,
 stops reading as soon as
it sees the closing  of a JSON object. If a server sends a JSON response
terminated with a newline, decoding the JSON object will leave the newline still
unread. The connection cannot be reused for a new request until that newline is
consumed. Thus, a healthy connection might end up being abandoned, even though
it was only a single byte away from being reusable.The unread body problem has been a long-standing sharp edge. Over the years,
many Go developers came up with their own workarounds. Most commonly, this is
done by draining the response body prior to closing it:func getStatus(url string) (int, error) {
    resp, err := http.Get(url)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    drainAndCloseBody(resp.Body)
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}

func drainAndCloseBody(body io.ReadCloser) {
    io.Copy(io.Discard, body) // Drain the response body before closing it.
    body.Close()
}
While this works for small responses, if the unread body is large, establishing
a new TCP connection can be faster than downloading and discarding the rest of
the body. A better approach is to cap the number of bytes we are willing to
drain:func drainAndCloseBody(body io.ReadCloser) {
    io.CopyN(io.Discard, body, 256<<10) // Only drain up to 256 KB.
    body.Close()
}
However, a server might send a response body that trickles in slowly, so we also
need to cap the time we spend draining:func drainAndCloseBody(body io.ReadCloser) {
    drainDone := make(chan struct{}, 1)
    go func() {
        io.CopyN(io.Discard, body, 256<<10) // Only drain up to 256 KB.
        drainDone <- struct{}{}
    }()
    // Only spend at most 50 ms trying to drain the response body.
    select {
    case <-drainDone:
    case <-time.After(50 * time.Millisecond):
        // If we reach the 50 ms timeout, body.Close will run and interrupt the
        // io.CopyN, preventing goroutine leaks.
    }
    body.Close()
}
Of course, we also do not want to block for up to 50 milliseconds when closing a
response body. Instead, we can drain the body asynchronously in the background
and let it race against subsequent requests:func drainAndCloseBody(body io.ReadCloser) {
    drainDone := make(chan struct{}, 1)
    go func() {
        io.CopyN(io.Discard, body, 256<<10) // Only drain up to 256 KB.
        drainDone <- struct{}{}
    }()
    go func() {
        // Only spend at most 50 ms trying to drain the response body.
        select {
        case <-drainDone:
            // If the drain can finish before a new request is sent, the
            // connection will be reused; otherwise, a new connection is
            // created, and the current connection will be put in a pool of
            // idle connections, potentially being reused for another future
            // request.
        case <-time.After(50 * time.Millisecond):
            // If we reach the 50 ms timeout, body.Close will run and interrupt
            // the io.CopyN, preventing goroutine leaks.
        }
        body.Close()
    }()
}
As we can see, draining response bodies safely is surprisingly tricky and
cumbersome. Worse, as the practice of draining response bodies spread, some
nuances were often lost. This has led to misunderstandings such as attempting to
drain response bodies for HTTP/2 connections, when doing so is unnecessary.The current state and conclusionStarting in Go 1.27, 
automatically does everything we have implemented in the code above: it will
asynchronously drain an unread HTTP/1 response body for up to 50 ms or 256 KB.
It also implements additional optimizations; for example, when a response
contains the  header, draining the body is guaranteed to be
wasted work, and it therefore skips doing so. Note that this behavior might
evolve in future versions of Go, as other possible enhancements become apparent.As a general best practice, we now recommend that users simply close their HTTP
bodies once they no longer need them, like in our original example:func getStatus(url string) (int, error) {
    resp, err := http.Get(url)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    defer resp.Body.Close()
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}
Go is all about simplicity. Let your code stay clean and simple, and let
 deal with the complexities on your behalf.
